{"cuisine/cuisine-of-north-dakota":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-north-dakota","n":"Cuisine of North Dakota","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Midwestern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-midwestern-united-states"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["The cuisine of North Dakota differs from average Midwestern cuisine in a number of ways.  Though much of the Midwest has strong  German influences, North Dakota also has a strong influence from Norway as well as the many ethnic Germans from Russia  who settled there.  There is also a strong Native American influence on the cuisine of North Dakota. Plants used as food by Native Americans are described in the North Dakota Ethnobotany database."],["As in the Midwest as a whole, meals are typically served in a smorgasbord format rather than as courses."],["Churches throughout the state commonly host annual fellowship dinners open to the community.  Perhaps one of the largest authentic Norwegian dinners is the annual lutefisk dinner hosted by the First Lutheran Church, Williston, North Dakota, every February."],["The largest Scandinavian Festival in North America is the annual Norsk Høstfest held every October, in Minot, North Dakota.  This five-day cultural event features Scandinavian dishes (Norway, Sweden, Denmark, Finland and Iceland), but does accommodate those who are not fond of lutefisk by providing many other choices of ethnic foods."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-north-dakota"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_North_Dakota"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Midwestern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-midwestern-united-states"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Midwestern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-midwestern-united-states"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/7d/United_Tribes_Technical_College_community_engagement_event_in_Bismarck%2C_North_Dakota_co-hosted_by_USDA_on_May_31%2C_2023_41.jpg/500px-United_Tribes_Technical_College_community_engagement_event_in_Bismarck%2C_North_Dakota_co-hosted_by_USDA_on_May_31%2C_2023_41.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"USDAgov","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:United_Tribes_Technical_College_community_engagement_event_in_Bismarck%2C_North_Dakota_co-hosted_by_USDA_on_May_31%2C_2023_41.jpg"}},"cuisine/culture-of-baltimore-food":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/culture-of-baltimore-food","n":"Cuisine of Baltimore","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine of the Mid-Atlantic United States","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mid-atlantic-united-states"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["The city of Baltimore, Maryland, has been a predominantly working-class town through much of its history with several surrounding affluent suburbs and, being found in a Mid-Atlantic state but south of the Mason–Dixon line, can lay claim to a blend of Northern and Southern American traditions."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"culture-of-baltimore-food"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_of_Baltimore#Food"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Cuisine of the Mid-Atlantic United States","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mid-atlantic-united-states"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Chesapeake Bay Cooking with John Shields","ref":"cookbook/chesapeake-bay-cooking-with-john-shields","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"John Shields, 1998","note":"Crab, oyster, and other Chesapeake dishes from a Baltimore chef and TV host.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780767900287","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780767900287"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Cuisine of the Mid-Atlantic United States","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mid-atlantic-united-states"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/07/Guilford.jpg/500px-Guilford.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Unknown author","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guilford.jpg"}},"cuisine/chinese-islamic-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/chinese-islamic-cuisine","n":"Chinese Islamic cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"],["Islamic diet","cuisine/islamic-dietary-laws"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["Chinese Islamic cuisine consists of variations of regionally popular foods that are typical of Han Chinese cuisine, in particular to make them halal. Dishes borrow ingredients from Middle Eastern, Turkic, Iranian and South Asian cuisines, notably mutton and spices. Much like other northern Chinese cuisines, Chinese Islamic cuisine uses wheat noodles as the staple, rather than rice. Chinese Islamic dishes include clear-broth beef noodle soup and chuanr."],["The Hui (Chinese-speaking Muslims), Bonan, Dongxiang, Salar and Uyghurs of China, as well as the Dungans of Central Asia and the Panthays of Burma, collectively contribute to Chinese Islamic cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"chinese-islamic-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Islamic_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"],["Islamic diet","cuisine/islamic-dietary-laws"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"],["Islamic diet","cuisine/islamic-dietary-laws"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/84/5838-Linxia-City-Tuanjie-Lu-halal-bakery.jpg/500px-5838-Linxia-City-Tuanjie-Lu-halal-bakery.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"User:Vmenkov","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:5838-Linxia-City-Tuanjie-Lu-halal-bakery.jpg"}},"cuisine/yamal-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/yamal-cuisine","n":"Yamal cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Russia","intro":[["Traditional Yamal cuisine is  an important part of national culture of the Nenets, Khanty and Komi. Yamal is a hunting and fishing land so many dishes include meat, fowl and fish.  Berries and mushrooms are also abundant; this accounts for the wealth of \"gifts of the forests\" on the Yamal table. Presently, Yamal cuisine is renowned for diverse delicacies of Russian cuisine. Yamal hosts enjoy treating their guests with traditional Yamal cuisine and have hot tea which came to Yamal cuisine from Russia. Tea has become the national Yamal beverage, drunk to warm and soothe. Yamal is in northern Russia with frigid winters, so its cuisine should be nourishing to give the people energy to survive during the long  winter time. The essential component of Yamal cuisine is warm and hot dishes. Fresh fruits and vegetables are rarely used as they must be imported, as they are not grown in the tundra. The Yamal cuisine is associated with malosol and stroganina. Yamal cuisine has absorbed techniques from other regions. Yamal cuisine is based on reindeer meat and fish of the Yamal rivers, enriched with mushrooms and bread."],["The most known dishes of Yamal cuisine are:"],["Fish Soup made from burbot, Malosol, Bread on the Selkup, Stroganina (Sliced Fish), Chopsy Shomoh, Warka, Sliced meat, Preserves of vendace, Kulebyaka (pie with fish), and Aibat-nyarhul (raw fish)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"yamal-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yamal_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/tsimshian-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/tsimshian-foodways","n":"Tsimshian foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Annette Island (Metlakatla), Southeast Alaska","names":"Tsimshian (Ts'msyen)","intro":[["In Alaska, the Tsimshian live mainly in Metlakatla on Annette Island, where they moved from British Columbia in 1887. Their food comes from the sea and the forest, with ",["Salmon","ingredient/salmon"],", halibut, shellfish, seaweed and ",["Eulachon","ingredient/eulachon"]," at its heart."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Alaska Native Heritage Center","url":"https://www.alaskanative.net/"},{"name":"University of Alaska Museum of the North, Alaska Native cultures guide","url":"https://uaf.edu/museum/education/docents/pdfs/NC2-3_Intro_2014.pdf"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"tsimshian-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"ingredient/sesame":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/sesame","n":"Sesame","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 7 dishes across 5 cuisines","intro":[["Sesame is a plant in the genus Sesamum, also called benne. Numerous wild relatives occur in Africa and a smaller number in India. It is widely naturalized in tropical regions around the world and is cultivated for its edible seeds, which grow in pods. World production in 2024 was 6.7 million tonnes (7.4 million short tons), with India, Myanmar, and Sudan as the largest producers."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-sesame"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sesame"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":5,"groups":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Black sesame roll","dish/black-sesame-roll"],["Dandan noodles","dish/dandan-noodles"],["Huangqiao sesame cake","dish/huangqiao-sesame-cake"],["Mianwo","dish/mianwo"]]],["Hubei cuisine","cuisine/hubei-cuisine",[["Mianwo","dish/mianwo"]]],["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine",[["Handvo","dish/handvo"]]],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Jian dui","dish/jian-dui"]]],["Taiwan cuisine","cuisine/taiwan-cuisine",[["Kiâm-piánn","dish/kiam-piann"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Sesame seed","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL","world":6268018,"top":[["Sudan","cuisine/sudanese-cuisine",929775],["India","cuisine/indian-cuisine",733762],["Myanmar","cuisine/burmese-cuisine",710550],["Ethiopia","cuisine/ethiopian-cuisine",443989],["China","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",436200],["Nigeria","cuisine/nigerian-cuisine",391085],["Burkina Faso","cuisine/burkinabe-cuisine",259843],["Pakistan","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine",223955],["Chad","cuisine/chadian-cuisine",220434],["Tanzania","cuisine/tanzanian-cuisine",216783],["Brazil","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine",214420],["Central African Rep.","cuisine/centrafrican-cuisine",191452],["S. Sudan","cuisine/south-sudanese-cuisine",176989],["Mozambique","cuisine/mozambican-cuisine",161415],["Uganda","cuisine/ugandan-cuisine",141200]]}},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Lard","ingredient/lard"],["Chili oil","ingredient/chili-oil"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Chinese noodles","ingredient/chinese-noodles"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Mala","ingredient/mala"],["Noodles","ingredient/noodles"],["Bottle gourd","ingredient/bottle-gourd"],["Lentils","ingredient/lentils"],["Alkali","ingredient/alkali"],["Caramel","ingredient/caramel"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Suet","ingredient/suet"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/7/70/Sesamum_indicum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-129.jpg","w